            ]
          }
        ],
        "learning": {
          "phase": "revise",
          "choice": "1",
          "attempt": "At 50% use I valued 10 saved hours at $24 and called $240 cash savings. I thought the $240 tool expense made the cash result zero.",
          "revision": "At 30% use I get 6 hours released and $144 value, minus $240 expense. I now think the monthly cash change is -$96.",
          "transfer": ""
        }
      },
      "status": 200,
      "streamHeader": "v1",
      "response": "Your arithmetic is correct: 30% of 1,200 is 360 replies, which releases 6 hours, valued at $144. The misconception is the last step. The -$96 is a **net capacity value**, which compares valued time with the new expense. It is not a cash change.\n\nCash moves only when actual spending or receipts change. Here payroll is unchanged, so no one is paid less, and the released time is only potential capacity. The $240 recurring tool and support cost is a real cash outflow. The monthly cash change is therefore **-$240**, and the $144 is a value estimate, not money.\n\nThis also affects your 50% answer: the saving was $0 net capacity value, and the cash change was -$240, not a $240 saving. The decision implication still holds: at 30% use the value case is weaker, so a bounded test should measure actual use and complete effort before any broader commitment. Your course reading on cash flows is in the [unit lab](/teaching/ai-solutions/unit-08.html#ta-lab-8).\n\n**Question:** If the operation later cut $144 a month of overtime because of the released hours, which of your three numbers would change, and what would the monthly cash change become?",
